  "query_preview": "..Dry and mild conditions continue overnight and \nthrough a majority of Monday under increasing southwesterly flow \naloft ahead of our next weather system. High-res guidance\nindicates showery precipitation beginning across southwest Utah as\nearly as Monday evening, with the bulk of moisture moving in mid-\nto-late Tuesday morning as the associated upper-level shortwave\ntrough pivots into the Great Basin. Expect widespread\nprecipitation to overspread the region with this moisture surge \nTuesday morning. A lull in activity is expected by early\nafternoon, ahead of a cold front passage late Tuesday afternoon \ninto the evening. A quick-moving band of heavy precipitation is \nexpected to accompany this frontal passage, with heaviest \nprecipitation rates favored mainly across northern Utah and \nsouthwest Wyoming where dynamics will be maximized. High- res \nguidance indicates a more disorganized, spotty line further south \ninto central and southern Utah late Tuesday. Shower activity will\nquickly dwindle behind the frontal passage, with most activity \nwrapped up by Tuesday night.\n\nSnow levels will remain around 8000-9000 feet through a majority \nof this storm, though snow levels drop to around 5500-6000 feet \nbehind the cold front passage late Tuesday. As such, snow\naccumulations are favored for Utah's mountains, particularly those\nabove 8000 feet. Caution is advised for those traveling across\nhigh-elevation routes through this period, as accumulating snow is\nexpected to bring dangerous travel conditions.\n\nA brief break in active weather is expected on Wednesday as a\nshortwave ridge traverses the region ahead of our next storm. Of \nnote, however, is the potential to finally reach a low of 32F or \nless here in SLC on Wednesday morning; current guidance indicates \na 90% chance. This would be the first time SLC observes freezing \ntemperatures this season.\n\nOur late-week storm is still on track to be colder and longer\nduration, impacting the region beginning early Thursday through at\nleast Friday night. There still remains a bit of uncertainty on \nhow quickly the colder airmass moves into the region, however,\nwith ensembles indicating H7 temperatures ranging between -3 to \n-8C Thursday afternoon. About 20% of models indicate this colder \nscenario that could bring snow levels close to valley floors \nThursday night, while the rest indicate snow levels remaining more\nelevated through this period. This H7 temperature spread \ndecreases significantly on Friday morning, with models in good \nagreement in temperatures around -8 to -9C, indicative of snow \nlevels near or below valley floors. All said, this system is \nlooking like it will bring more substantial snowfall to all Utah \nmountains between Thursday and Friday. The one key uncertainty at \nthis point is if snow levels can drop quickly enough to result in \nmeasurable snow down to valley levels. Will remain something to \nmonitor over the next few days.",
